WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:09.714
250 years ago,

00:00:09.756 --> 00:00:11.550
just under a mile from here,

00:00:11.591 --> 00:00:13.343
Thomas Jefferson wrote

00:00:13.385 --> 00:00:16.555
one of the most consequential documents

00:00:16.555 --> 00:00:18.014
in American history.

00:00:18.473 --> 00:00:23.395
Now, the Declaration
of Independence wasn't perfect,

00:00:23.436 --> 00:00:24.938
just like the guy who wrote it,

00:00:24.938 --> 00:00:27.732
and the country it created
weren't perfect either.

00:00:28.608 --> 00:00:31.403
But it set us on this path, right?

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:38.827
This beautiful, audacious,
complicated path that's still evolving.

00:00:40.453 --> 00:00:44.291
And the Declaration of Independence
still gives me chills.

00:00:45.375 --> 00:00:49.671
“We hold these truths to be self-evident,

00:00:49.671 --> 00:00:52.716
that all are created equal,

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:56.428
that they are endowed
with certain unalienable rights,

00:00:56.469 --> 00:00:59.055
and that among these are life,

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:02.601
liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.”

00:01:02.642 --> 00:01:04.227
It's good, right?

00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:05.395
(Laughter)

00:01:05.937 --> 00:01:07.272
Did you get chills?

00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:08.440
(Laughter)

00:01:09.232 --> 00:01:12.027
I mean, yeah,
I changed it a little, OK, from --

00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:13.570
(Laughter)

00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:15.947
"All men are created equal"

00:01:15.989 --> 00:01:17.657
to "all are created equal"

00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:19.743
because it makes it better, right?

00:01:19.784 --> 00:01:21.119
Thank you.

00:01:21.536 --> 00:01:23.830
(Applause)

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.958
And that's the point I want
to talk with you about today,

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.252
that to live up to its promise,

00:01:29.294 --> 00:01:33.214
we have evolved democracy in the past

00:01:33.214 --> 00:01:36.176
and it needs to evolve again,

00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:42.057
not only so that we can feel
our votes count,

00:01:42.098 --> 00:01:48.271
but so we can count
democracy's real benefits

00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:50.023
in our lives,

00:01:50.065 --> 00:01:51.816
including in our wallets.

00:01:53.068 --> 00:01:54.402
So a couple of years ago,

00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:57.447
a philanthropic foundation
in the United States

00:01:57.447 --> 00:01:59.032
asked me to do some research

00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:04.037
to find out why authoritarianism
was gaining ground around the globe.

00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:06.414
So I interviewed

00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:10.502
civic leaders in West Africa
and scholars in Eastern Europe

00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:12.962
and democracy leaders in Latin America.

00:02:12.962 --> 00:02:14.172
And I asked them,

00:02:14.172 --> 00:02:16.841
What's authoritarianism
getting so right?

00:02:17.384 --> 00:02:21.888
These leaders that are pushing people
and places away from democracy,

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:26.184
what are these authoritarians
doing that's so effective?

00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:31.189
And pretty much everyone I talked to
said I was asking the wrong question --

00:02:31.856 --> 00:02:37.612
that authoritarianism wasn’t surging
because of anything it was doing right,

00:02:37.654 --> 00:02:40.782
but because of what
democracy was getting wrong.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:44.911
That stopped me in my tracks.

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:46.788
The way they put it,

00:02:46.788 --> 00:02:49.457
authoritarianism was like
an opportunistic disease

00:02:49.499 --> 00:02:53.837
that could only take root
when democracy was weak.

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:56.756
And in case you
haven't been paying attention --

00:02:56.756 --> 00:02:57.924
(Laughter)

00:02:59.342 --> 00:03:01.594
Democracy is kind of on life support.

00:03:01.636 --> 00:03:04.639
So according to Freedom House,

00:03:04.681 --> 00:03:09.352
only about 20 percent
of the world's population

00:03:09.352 --> 00:03:12.939
lives in a country currently
that can be considered free.

00:03:13.565 --> 00:03:18.528
And dissatisfaction
with democracy is rising worldwide.

00:03:18.528 --> 00:03:21.239
In the US, for instance,

00:03:21.281 --> 00:03:25.493
about three out of five Americans
say they are currently dissatisfied

00:03:25.535 --> 00:03:27.704
with how democracy is working.

00:03:27.704 --> 00:03:28.955
Why?

00:03:29.956 --> 00:03:33.501
The answer came from a conversation
I had with Kwasi Prempeh.

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:37.380
So Kwasi runs the Ghana Center
for Democratic Development.

00:03:37.422 --> 00:03:42.218
Now Ghana only won independence
from colonial rule in 1957,

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.846
so not that long ago,

00:03:44.888 --> 00:03:48.767
but long enough that most
people in Ghana today

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:52.187
don't remember a time before democracy.

00:03:53.813 --> 00:03:56.941
But what they do know

00:03:56.983 --> 00:04:03.823
is that they're struggling economically
right now under democracy.

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:10.038
And so, as Kwasi put it,
they very understandably ask,

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:14.584
"What has democracy done for me lately?"

00:04:16.377 --> 00:04:18.213
His point

00:04:18.213 --> 00:04:23.384
is that democracies that do not
deliver broad-based opportunity

00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:26.930
are vulnerable to autocratic capture.

00:04:28.306 --> 00:04:30.016
And research backs this up.

00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:34.395
So Susan Stokes is a political science
professor at the University of Chicago.

00:04:34.395 --> 00:04:40.693
She has looked at variables for why
democracy erodes in some countries,

00:04:40.735 --> 00:04:42.111
but not others.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.824
Income inequality

00:04:45.865 --> 00:04:50.995
is the overwhelming variable
in democratic backsliding,

00:04:51.037 --> 00:04:54.707
so much so that Stokes said
short of unplugging their computers,

00:04:54.749 --> 00:04:56.251
they couldn't make it go away.

00:04:56.292 --> 00:04:58.962
Now I want to be really clear
about something.

00:04:58.962 --> 00:05:02.340
In the richest country in the world,

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:06.761
a country that boasts about being
a land of opportunity for all,

00:05:06.803 --> 00:05:11.850
ensuring meaningful economic
opportunity for all

00:05:11.891 --> 00:05:13.560
is the right thing to do.

00:05:14.269 --> 00:05:17.689
It is the moral thing to do,

00:05:17.689 --> 00:05:20.358
and that is enough of a reason to do it.

00:05:21.609 --> 00:05:23.695
And it's practical.

00:05:26.364 --> 00:05:30.702
Economic justice is how we save democracy.

00:05:31.452 --> 00:05:38.418
(Applause)

00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:43.840
So I'm feeling like this might be
as much of a revelation to you

00:05:43.882 --> 00:05:45.008
as it was to me,

00:05:45.008 --> 00:05:47.427
or at least it was maybe
more of a revelation to me

00:05:47.468 --> 00:05:48.636
than it was to you,

00:05:48.678 --> 00:05:53.266
because we were used to,
we have, for our 250 years,

00:05:53.308 --> 00:05:56.519
thought of democracy
and economic opportunity

00:05:56.561 --> 00:05:58.897
as distinct and separate.

00:05:58.938 --> 00:06:00.607
I mean, obviously interrelated,

00:06:00.648 --> 00:06:02.734
and for many of us equally important,

00:06:02.775 --> 00:06:04.110
but separate.

00:06:04.777 --> 00:06:05.987
They're not.

00:06:06.654 --> 00:06:09.407
They're two sides of the same coin.

00:06:10.617 --> 00:06:14.454
Because if democracy doesn't deliver

00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:17.874
meaningful economic opportunity,

00:06:17.916 --> 00:06:21.419
democracy starts to feel meaningless.

00:06:22.545 --> 00:06:26.341
And that makes authoritarianism and other
forms of government more attractive,

00:06:26.382 --> 00:06:29.052
not in and of themselves,

00:06:29.093 --> 00:06:31.262
but by default,

00:06:31.304 --> 00:06:36.017
because democracy
is defaulting on its promise.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:41.356
So I said before that we've evolved
democracy in the past,

00:06:41.356 --> 00:06:42.899
and it needs to evolve again.

00:06:42.899 --> 00:06:45.485
I want us to think
about democracy in phases.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:50.949
Democracy 1.0 is that initial idea

00:06:50.949 --> 00:06:53.034
that the people

00:06:53.076 --> 00:06:56.037
should have the power
in a political system,

00:06:56.079 --> 00:06:58.331
not kings,

00:06:58.373 --> 00:06:59.874
not empires,

00:06:59.916 --> 00:07:01.834
not corporations,

00:07:01.834 --> 00:07:03.002
the people.

00:07:03.586 --> 00:07:05.755
It was a radical idea for its time.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:08.758
Honestly, it's still really radical.

00:07:09.342 --> 00:07:11.719
But as we know,
that was just the beginning.

00:07:11.761 --> 00:07:16.057
Democracy 2.0

00:07:16.099 --> 00:07:18.434
is the phase where we expand

00:07:18.476 --> 00:07:23.106
who is included in democracy,
who gets to participate,

00:07:23.106 --> 00:07:25.858
who counts as the people.

00:07:27.485 --> 00:07:29.612
In the United States,

00:07:29.612 --> 00:07:31.823
that process has been long.

00:07:32.365 --> 00:07:35.743
It has taken generations of struggle --

00:07:35.785 --> 00:07:38.871
abolition, suffrage, civil rights --

00:07:38.913 --> 00:07:40.707
to make democracy more inclusive,

00:07:40.707 --> 00:07:45.336
to say that everyone gets an equal vote

00:07:45.336 --> 00:07:48.423
because everyone matters equally.

00:07:50.049 --> 00:07:53.469
The brilliant scholar Isabel Wilkerson
argues that, in fact,

00:07:53.511 --> 00:07:57.640
the US wasn't even really
a democracy until 1965,

00:07:57.682 --> 00:07:59.809
when the Voting Rights Act became law.

00:07:59.851 --> 00:08:04.439
And ever since then there has been
an aggressive movement

00:08:04.439 --> 00:08:06.357
to roll back that progress.

00:08:06.816 --> 00:08:12.572
While we should be making
more progress on democracy 2.0,

00:08:12.613 --> 00:08:13.781
far-right extremists,

00:08:13.823 --> 00:08:17.702
aided and abetted by the Supreme Court,

00:08:17.744 --> 00:08:20.204
have dismantled the Voting Rights Act

00:08:20.246 --> 00:08:23.207
and are trying to take us backwards.

00:08:24.959 --> 00:08:27.628
So realistically,

00:08:27.628 --> 00:08:29.464
while it makes sense to question

00:08:29.505 --> 00:08:33.634
whether we even have a democracy
right now in the United States,

00:08:33.676 --> 00:08:35.887
either way,

00:08:35.928 --> 00:08:40.141
we still tend to focus more

00:08:40.183 --> 00:08:44.353
on democracy as a process

00:08:44.353 --> 00:08:49.400
and not enough on what democracy produces.

00:08:50.318 --> 00:08:52.737
On democratic outcomes.

00:08:53.654 --> 00:08:55.615
We think and argue and talk a lot

00:08:55.615 --> 00:08:59.410
about who gets
to be included in democracy,

00:08:59.452 --> 00:09:01.996
but not enough about why.

00:09:03.456 --> 00:09:06.793
Why do we have a democracy
in the first place?

00:09:08.252 --> 00:09:10.379
Answering that question --

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:15.343
defining the goal of democracy --

00:09:15.384 --> 00:09:19.597
that's democracy 3.0.

00:09:20.807 --> 00:09:26.104
Democracy 3.0 says

00:09:26.104 --> 00:09:32.026
that if all of us
were truly created equal,

00:09:32.068 --> 00:09:35.738
and if all of us truly get

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:39.325
to participate equally,

00:09:39.367 --> 00:09:42.870
then the outcomes of our democracy

00:09:42.912 --> 00:09:47.250
should be more equitable, too, right?

00:09:47.667 --> 00:09:51.838
More widely shared
and benefiting everyone.

00:09:53.422 --> 00:09:59.011
(Applause)

00:09:59.887 --> 00:10:01.097
But --

00:10:01.097 --> 00:10:02.265
(Laughter)

00:10:02.682 --> 00:10:03.850
Did you hear it coming?

00:10:03.891 --> 00:10:04.892
(Laughter)

00:10:04.892 --> 00:10:06.102
'Cause there's a but.

00:10:07.854 --> 00:10:11.566
In the US today, your zip code
predicts your life expectancy,

00:10:11.607 --> 00:10:13.401
your race, your wealth.

00:10:13.442 --> 00:10:16.320
And your parents’ income
predicts your future.

00:10:16.988 --> 00:10:18.489
That ain't democratic.

00:10:19.532 --> 00:10:20.700
And it's getting worse.

00:10:20.741 --> 00:10:25.496
The top one percent of Americans
control as much wealth

00:10:25.496 --> 00:10:32.420
as the entire bottom 90 percent
of the country combined.

00:10:33.921 --> 00:10:35.590
And according to one source,

00:10:35.631 --> 00:10:42.513
CEOs now make 281 times more

00:10:42.513 --> 00:10:44.974
than average workers.

00:10:45.016 --> 00:10:49.270
281 times more?

00:10:49.979 --> 00:10:52.982
What the hell is wrong with us?

00:10:53.024 --> 00:10:57.695
Are CEOs 281 times more talented?

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:02.116
Are they 281 times more deserving?

00:11:02.116 --> 00:11:06.996
Are they 281 times harder-working
than the rest of us?

00:11:07.788 --> 00:11:11.375
Look, we can use the word democracy
all we like, but let's be clear.

00:11:11.375 --> 00:11:17.715
We've really got more
of an oligarchy or a kleptocracy.

00:11:19.175 --> 00:11:22.720
Or maybe a monarchy
where the kings are billionaires

00:11:22.762 --> 00:11:26.265
and reality TV stars,
or sometimes both.

00:11:26.307 --> 00:11:28.309
(Laughter)

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:35.691
Democracy means

00:11:35.691 --> 00:11:39.654
that the kids of house
cleaners and mechanics

00:11:39.654 --> 00:11:43.950
have the same opportunities in life

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:46.327
as the kids of lawyers and tech execs.

00:11:46.369 --> 00:11:49.789
Democracy means that if you work hard,

00:11:49.789 --> 00:11:52.708
you can feed your family
and afford a place to live.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.335
Democracy means

00:11:54.335 --> 00:11:58.005
that power and profit

00:11:58.047 --> 00:12:00.883
are distributed democratically.

00:12:00.883 --> 00:12:04.595
That is democracy 3.0.

00:12:05.388 --> 00:12:10.268
(Applause)

00:12:11.435 --> 00:12:13.729
So for those who maybe didn't clap --

00:12:13.729 --> 00:12:17.400
(Laughter)

00:12:18.734 --> 00:12:21.279
This may be the part in the talk

00:12:21.279 --> 00:12:23.823
where some of y'all are getting
a little bit nervous.

00:12:23.864 --> 00:12:25.908
You hear "economic democracy"

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.662
and you think it means enforced sameness.

00:12:29.704 --> 00:12:30.997
It does not.

00:12:31.706 --> 00:12:36.043
Democracy 3.0 doesn't mean
everybody gets an equal house

00:12:36.043 --> 00:12:38.921
and an equal income and equal outcomes.

00:12:40.715 --> 00:12:45.553
Democracy 3.0 simply means

00:12:45.594 --> 00:12:48.889
that everyone gets an equal chance

00:12:48.931 --> 00:12:51.559
at the American dream.

00:12:51.559 --> 00:12:53.144
Are you with me?

00:12:53.144 --> 00:12:57.064
(Applause)

00:12:57.064 --> 00:13:01.902
OK, so how do we actually get
to democracy 3.0?

00:13:01.944 --> 00:13:04.322
Well, the first thing
is a shift in mindset.

00:13:04.322 --> 00:13:06.449
To start seeing political democracy

00:13:06.449 --> 00:13:10.119
and economic democracy
as mutually intertwined,

00:13:10.161 --> 00:13:14.665
which means we need
to advance economic justice

00:13:14.707 --> 00:13:19.211
not only for basic fairness, right?

00:13:19.253 --> 00:13:22.089
But because that’s the way
we strengthen democracy.

00:13:22.465 --> 00:13:23.883
And on a practical level,

00:13:23.924 --> 00:13:26.594
it means that, yeah,
to strengthen democracy,

00:13:26.635 --> 00:13:32.433
we need to restore and strengthen
the Voting Rights Act,

00:13:32.475 --> 00:13:37.855
but also we need to raise wages,

00:13:37.897 --> 00:13:39.982
and we need to get money out of politics,

00:13:40.024 --> 00:13:42.735
and we need to tax wealth more fairly.

00:13:42.735 --> 00:13:44.945
(Applause)

00:13:44.945 --> 00:13:46.155
And --

00:13:46.155 --> 00:13:47.865
(Applause)

00:13:47.907 --> 00:13:52.036
And democracy 3.0 means

00:13:52.078 --> 00:13:56.791
that just like voters should have
equal voice and power,

00:13:56.832 --> 00:14:00.503
workers should have equal voice and power,

00:14:00.544 --> 00:14:04.131
and democracy at work means unions.

00:14:04.173 --> 00:14:07.051
(Applause and cheers)

00:14:07.093 --> 00:14:13.766
In fact, the first wisp of democracy 3.0
was at the last turn of the century,

00:14:13.808 --> 00:14:19.230
the last time our country got fed up
with extreme wealth and extreme poverty.

00:14:19.647 --> 00:14:23.150
That's why we created unions,
which helped create the middle class.

00:14:23.192 --> 00:14:26.570
And then we created a social safety net,

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.490
things like Social Security
and eventually Medicare.

00:14:29.532 --> 00:14:32.660
Taxing wealth to pay for programs

00:14:32.701 --> 00:14:36.705
that would prevent
extreme poverty and suffering.

00:14:36.747 --> 00:14:39.667
We just need to do it again --

00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:41.794
plus do more --

00:14:41.836 --> 00:14:45.881
things like universal health care for all.

00:14:45.881 --> 00:14:47.842
(Applause)

00:14:47.842 --> 00:14:52.930
And more democratic ways
of owning businesses,

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:55.266
like employee ownership.

00:14:55.266 --> 00:14:57.935
And we need to make sure

00:14:57.935 --> 00:15:02.398
that new technologies
actually improve our lives

00:15:02.398 --> 00:15:05.151
instead of undermining them.

00:15:05.151 --> 00:15:09.655
(Cheers and applause)

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.240
Look.

00:15:12.616 --> 00:15:17.204
The threat to end our democracy

00:15:17.246 --> 00:15:20.499
also contains within it

00:15:20.541 --> 00:15:22.877
an invitation to make it better.

00:15:22.918 --> 00:15:25.254
But our vision cannot be

00:15:25.254 --> 00:15:29.300
just to go back to the way
things were a few elections ago.

00:15:30.009 --> 00:15:34.930
We have a moral and practical imperative

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:40.436
to future-proof democracy
by helping each other.

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.692
By delivering real political voice

00:15:46.692 --> 00:15:50.696
and real economic power

00:15:50.696 --> 00:15:53.657
to we, the people,

00:15:53.699 --> 00:15:54.825
all of us.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.038
Because if we don't,

00:15:59.079 --> 00:16:02.291
if democracy doesn't deliver
broad-based opportunity,

00:16:02.333 --> 00:16:05.669
people will eventually trade it
for something they think will.

00:16:06.420 --> 00:16:08.589
And history shows us what happens next.

00:16:09.465 --> 00:16:11.050
It's not good for any of us.

00:16:12.218 --> 00:16:17.723
Democracy contains within it
the tools for its own demise,

00:16:17.765 --> 00:16:19.308
but also its renewal.

00:16:21.644 --> 00:16:26.273
But if we want a better country,
a fairer country,

00:16:26.273 --> 00:16:28.150
we're going to have to fight for it.

00:16:28.651 --> 00:16:33.906
We, the people, demand

00:16:33.906 --> 00:16:39.870
that our politics
and our economy work way better

00:16:39.912 --> 00:16:43.457
for us and everyone around us,

00:16:43.457 --> 00:16:47.461
because democracy can be more

00:16:47.503 --> 00:16:52.216
than something we do
every couple of years in a voting booth,

00:16:52.216 --> 00:16:54.760
but something we can eat

00:16:54.802 --> 00:16:57.054
and something we can live in

00:16:57.054 --> 00:17:00.182
and something we can put in our pockets

00:17:00.224 --> 00:17:02.101
every single day.

00:17:02.768 --> 00:17:09.108
That is the only way we are going
to save ourselves,

00:17:09.149 --> 00:17:11.110
our country,

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:14.613
and the ideals we hold so dear.

00:17:16.156 --> 00:17:21.287
Democracy 1.0 gives life
to a radical idea.

00:17:22.037 --> 00:17:28.502
